{
  "concept": "Electrophilic bromination of benzene proceeds through a high first barrier to a carbocation intermediate, and then goes on to substitution — which restores aromaticity and ends downhill — rather than to addition, which destroys aromaticity and ends uphill.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Pale blue rectangular panel enclosing the entire energy diagram",
    "Vertical arrow at far left with a yellow-to-orange gradient, labeled 'Energy' in bold black rotated text",
    "Horizontal axis caption beneath the panel: 'Reaction progress' followed by a magenta right-pointing arrow",
    "Dashed horizontal reactant energy level on the left side",
    "Reactant structures on that level: benzene hexagon (with inscribed circle or alternating double bonds), black plus sign, 'Br2' in magenta",
    "Black substitution reaction curve: rises from reactant level to a tall first maximum, falls into a trough, rises to a lower second maximum, falls to a low product level",
    "Short dashed horizontal tick capping the first maximum (TS1)",
    "Vertical double-headed black arrow from first maximum down to reactant level, labeled 'ΔG‡'",
    "Trough intermediate structure: cyclohexadienyl cation — six-membered ring with two double bonds, sp3 carbon bearing an H and a magenta Br, with a '+' sign above the ring",
    "Second, lower maximum on the black curve (TS2)",
    "Dashed horizontal product energy level below the reactant level",
    "Small vertical double-headed arrow between reactant dashed level and product dashed level, labeled 'ΔG°'",
    "Product structures on the low level: benzene ring with magenta 'Br' substituent, black plus sign, 'H' in black with 'Br' in magenta",
    "Label 'Substitution' beneath the product structures",
    "Magenta addition curve branching from the same trough, rising over its own maximum and ending on a plateau ABOVE the reactant level",
    "Bracketed addition product: six-membered ring with one remaining double bond, two sp3 carbons each bearing an H (black) and a Br (magenta), enclosed in square brackets",
    "Labels 'Addition' and bold magenta '(does not occur)' beneath the bracketed structure",
    "Color legend cue: black = substitution pathway, magenta = bromine atoms / addition pathway"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "pathwayStage",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Walk the reaction coordinate",
      "states": [
        "reactants",
        "first transition state",
        "cationic intermediate",
        "second transition state",
        "products"
      ],
      "default": "reactants",
      "teaches": "Electrophilic aromatic bromination is a two-step process: a slow, high-barrier attack on the electrophile gives a carbocation intermediate, which then loses H+ over a much smaller second barrier."
    },
    {
      "id": "branchChoice",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Fate of the intermediate",
      "options": [
        "Substitution",
        "Addition"
      ],
      "default": "Substitution",
      "teaches": "From the same intermediate, substitution descends below the reactants because aromaticity is regained, while addition climbs above them because aromatic stabilization is permanently lost — so only substitution occurs."
    },
    {
      "id": "energyArrow",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "ΔG‡ measures the climb from reactants to the highest transition state (how fast), while ΔG° measures the net drop from reactants to products (how favorable) — two independent quantities on the same vertical scale."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Reactants on the baseline",
      "narration": "Benzene and Br2 sit on the left dashed level; everything else in the diagram is measured vertically against this line.",
      "state": {
        "pathwayStage": "reactants",
        "branchChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "The big first barrier",
      "narration": "The black curve climbs steeply to the first transition state. Breaking benzene's aromaticity to attack Br+ costs a large ΔG‡, which is why bromination needs a FeBr3 catalyst.",
      "state": {
        "pathwayStage": "first transition state",
        "branchChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "The carbocation intermediate",
      "narration": "The curve drops into a real minimum: the cyclohexadienyl cation, with H and Br on the same sp3 carbon and a delocalized positive charge. It is a genuine, if fleeting, species — not a transition state.",
      "state": {
        "pathwayStage": "cationic intermediate",
        "branchChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Second, smaller barrier",
      "narration": "Loss of H+ from the sp3 carbon crosses a much lower maximum — this step is fast compared with the first.",
      "state": {
        "pathwayStage": "second transition state",
        "branchChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Substitution ends downhill",
      "narration": "Bromobenzene plus HBr land below the reactant level: the aromatic ring is restored, so ΔG° is negative and the reaction is favorable.",
      "state": {
        "pathwayStage": "products",
        "branchChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "The road not taken: addition",
      "narration": "Switch the branch and the magenta curve leaves the same intermediate but finishes ABOVE the reactants — the dibromide has no aromatic stabilization, so addition is uphill and does not occur.",
      "state": {
        "pathwayStage": "products",
        "branchChoice": "Addition"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Both pathways share the same intermediate, so the choice is decided entirely by where they end: substitution regains the ~150 kJ/mol of aromatic stabilization and finishes below the reactants, while addition sacrifices it permanently and finishes above them.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide pale-blue panel with a thin black border. Far left, outside the panel, a tall upward arrow filled with a pale-yellow-to-orange vertical gradient, labeled 'Energy' in bold rotated black text. Below the panel, centered, 'Reaction progress' followed by a magenta arrow pointing right. Inside: a smooth black curve starting on a left dashed level at mid-low height, rising to a tall peak near the left third (peak capped by a short dashed tick), dipping to a shallow trough in the middle, rising again to a second, noticeably lower peak, then plunging to a dashed product level near the bottom right — clearly below the reactant dashed level. A magenta curve branches out of the same trough, rises to its own intermediate-height maximum just right of the black second peak, and levels off on a plateau above the reactant dashed level on the right. Structures drawn as line-angle skeletons: plain benzene hexagon plus magenta 'Br2' at lower left; in the trough, a cyclohexadienyl cation (ring with two double bonds, one sp3 carbon carrying black H and magenta Br, a '+' above); at the bottom right, bromobenzene with magenta Br plus black 'H' and magenta 'Br', captioned 'Substitution'; at the upper right inside square brackets, a ring with one double bond and two adjacent sp3 carbons each bearing black H and magenta Br, captioned 'Addition' with bold magenta '(does not occur)'. Annotation arrows: a long vertical double-headed black arrow from the first peak's dashed tick down to the reactant dashed level labeled 'ΔG‡'; a short vertical double-headed arrow between the reactant dashed level and the product dashed level labeled 'ΔG°'. Highlighting convention: active stage marker is a filled dot on the curve plus a glow around the corresponding structure; the inactive branch dims to light gray; clicking an energy arrow thickens it and extends its two dashed reference levels fully across the panel."
}

